    relevance: "Status codes, event kinds, and command words are classification problems: one value, many named cases. match/case (new in Python 3.10) states such tables directly — the code reads like the classification table in the analyst's notebook, which is exactly why it replaced long if/elif chains in modern codebases.",
    concept: [
      "match takes a value and tries each case pattern top to bottom; the first matching case runs, and no other does — the same first-match discipline as an if chain, but organised around ONE value's shape. Literal patterns (case 404:) match exact values. The wildcard case _: matches anything and plays the role of else: it belongs last, because everything below it would be unreachable.",
      "Patterns combine: case 401 | 403: means '401 or 403' — the or-pattern. A case can also CAPTURE: case code if code >= 500: binds the value to a name and adds a guard — an extra if that must also be true. Capturing plus guards is where match outgrows a simple lookup table: the pattern selects the family, the guard applies the rule.",
      "Choose honestly: for one value classified by constants, match is clearer; for unrelated tests on different values (locked AND attempts AND known_ip), an if chain remains the right tool — match does not replace it. You will also meet match used on tuples and dictionaries (structural patterns); this lesson's value patterns are the foundation those build on.",
    ],
    diagramTitle: "match, piece by piece",
    diagram: ["match status:        — one value under examination", "case 301 | 302:      — or-pattern: either literal matches", "case code if code >= 500:  — capture + guard: family, then rule", "case _:              — wildcard: everything else (always last)"],
    terms: [
      { term: "match statement", def: "Python 3.10+ statement that classifies a value by pattern, first match wins." },
      { term: "Or-pattern", def: "case 401 | 403: — several literal alternatives in one case." },
      { term: "Guard", def: "An extra if on a case: the pattern matches AND the guard must be true." },
      { term: "Wildcard", def: "case _: — matches anything; the match statement's else." },
    ],
    code: { lang: "python", title: "match_status.py — complete, runnable (Python 3.10+)", code: "def verdict(status):\n    match status:\n        case 200:\n            return \"OK - page served\"\n        case 301 | 302:\n            return \"Redirect - follow the Location header\"\n        case 401 | 403:\n            return \"Refused - authentication or authorization failed\"\n        case 404:\n            return \"Not found - check the path\"\n        case code if code >= 500:\n            return f\"Server error ({code}) - the problem is on their side\"\n        case _:\n            return f\"Unclassified status {status}\"\n\nfor code in [200, 302, 403, 404, 503, 418]:\n    print(code, \"->\", verdict(code))", output: "200 -> OK - page served\n302 -> Redirect - follow the Location header\n403 -> Refused - authentication or authorization failed\n404 -> Not found - check the path\n503 -> Server error (503) - the problem is on their side\n418 -> Unclassified status 418", lines: ["Each literal case names one row of the HTTP classification table — the code IS the table.", "301 | 302 shares one verdict: or-patterns group codes with the same meaning.", "case code if code >= 500 captures the value, then the guard tests it — that is how a whole family (all 5xx) gets one row.", "418 reaches the wildcard: match never falls through silently — the _ case makes 'unclassified' an explicit answer."] },
    mistakes: ["Putting case _ anywhere but last — everything below it becomes unreachable dead code.", "Using a bare name (case code:) expecting it to compare against a variable — a bare name CAPTURES (assigns), it does not compare.", "Reaching for match when the tests involve several different values — that is an if chain's job."],
    security: "Status-code and event-kind classification drives alerting: a 401/403 family means 'identity problem', 5xx means 'their side'. A missing wildcard means unclassified events vanish — the same honesty rule as the classifier's OTHER bucket, in new syntax.",
    pc: ["Run match_status.py, then add a case for 429 ('Too many requests - slow down, do not retry in a loop') in the right position.", "Rewrite verdict() as an if/elif chain and compare: which version would you rather review at 2 a.m.?"],
    android: ["Android: match needs Python 3.10+ — check your interpreter app's version (the pathlib lesson shows how) before practising there."],
