    code: { lang: "python", title: "tuples.py — complete, runnable (Python 3.10+)", code: "# A connection record is fixed-shape data: (host, port, service).\nconn = (\"192.168.56.10\", 22, \"ssh\")\nhost, port, service = conn            # unpacking: one name per slot\nprint(f\"{service} on {host}:{port}\")\n\n# Tuples are immutable - a record nobody can quietly edit.\ntry:\n    conn[1] = 2222\nexcept TypeError as e:\n    print(\"TypeError:\", e)\n\n# A function can return several values at once - really one tuple.\ndef scan_one(host):\n    return host, [22, 443], \"2 open ports\"\n\nname, open_ports, note = scan_one(\"192.168.56.10\")\nprint(name, open_ports, note)\n\n# Swap without a temp variable: tuple packing/unpacking does it.\na, b = \"closed\", \"open\"\na, b = b, a\nprint(\"After swap:\", a, b)\n\n# zip walks two lists together; enumerate adds the position.\nusers = [\"nadia\", \"rahim\"]\nfails = [3, 12]\nfor i, (user, count) in enumerate(zip(users, fails), start=1):\n    print(f\"{i}. {user}: {count}\")", output: "ssh on 192.168.56.10:22\nTypeError: 'tuple' object does not support item assignment\n192.168.56.10 [22, 443] 2 open ports\nAfter swap: open closed\n1. nadia: 3\n2. rahim: 12", lines: ["Unpacking names the slots once; afterwards the code says host and port, never conn[0] and conn[1].", "The TypeError is a guard rail: the record's shape is enforced by the type itself.", "scan_one 'returns three things' — really one tuple, unpacked by the caller into three names.", "enumerate(..., start=1) numbers rows the way humans count; zip stops at the SHORTER sequence — mismatched lengths silently drop the tail."] },
    mistakes: ["Writing a one-item tuple without the comma: (22) is just the int 22 in parentheses — a single tuple needs (22,).", "Unpacking with mismatched counts (three names, two values) — ValueError; use *rest when the tail is variable.", "Assuming zip complains about unequal lengths — it silently stops at the shorter one."],
    security: "Fixed records model evidence tuples — (timestamp, host, event) — whose shape must not drift between collection and report. Immutability is a small integrity control built into the language.",
    pc: ["Run tuples.py, then write a function describe(record) that unpacks a (user, fails, locked) tuple and prints a one-line verdict.", "Use zip to pair three IPs with three verdicts and print a numbered table."],
