    id: "l4-pym-sets", level: 4, title: "Sets: Uniqueness, Membership, and Set Operations",
    difficulty: "Foundation", minutes: 25, xp: 50, skill: "Python",
    prereq: "Lesson: Dictionaries in Depth.",
    objectives: ["Create sets and explain why duplicates collapse", "Use in for fast membership tests", "Compute intersection, difference, union, and symmetric difference", "Test subsets with <= and deduplicate collections"],
    relevance: "'Which users appeared on BOTH days?', 'who is NEW today?', 'have we seen this IP before?' — set questions are investigation questions. A set answers them in one operator each, and its membership test stays fast at a million entries, where a list scan slows to a crawl.",
    concept: [
      "A set is an unordered collection of unique items: {'nadia', 'rahim', 'nadia'} holds two entries — the duplicate collapses on entry. Sets have no indexes (no monday[0]) because order is not their promise; their promise is uniqueness and membership. 'x' in a_set is a near-instant hash lookup, the same machinery as dictionary keys (a set is, in effect, keys without values).",
      "The operations mirror the analyst's questions exactly: a & b (intersection — in both), a - b (difference — in a, not in b: the NEW-on-Tuesday set), a | b (union — in either), a ^ b (symmetric difference — in exactly one). subset tests read as mathematics: flagged <= known asks whether every flagged user is a known user.",
      "The everyday job is deduplication: set(a_list) collapses repeats — 4 sightings become 2 unique IPs. Convert back with sorted(set(...)) when a stable, readable order is needed for a report, because a set's own iteration order is not a display order.",
    ],
    diagramTitle: "Set operations are investigation questions",
    diagram: ["a & b   intersection        — who appeared on BOTH days?", "a - b   difference          — who is NEW in a?", "a | b   union               — everyone seen in either", "a ^ b   symmetric diff.     — who appeared on exactly ONE day?"],
