    code: { lang: "python", title: "sets.py — complete, runnable (Python 3.10+)", code: "monday = {\"nadia\", \"rahim\", \"admin\", \"nadia\"}   # duplicate collapses\ntuesday = {\"rahim\", \"sana\", \"admin\"}\nprint(\"Monday users:\", sorted(monday))\nprint(\"Both days:\", sorted(monday & tuesday))        # intersection\nprint(\"New on Tuesday:\", sorted(tuesday - monday))   # difference\nprint(\"Only one day:\", sorted(monday ^ tuesday))     # symmetric difference\nprint(\"Was 'guest' seen?\", \"guest\" in monday)\n\n# The classic set job: deduplicate while keeping it a collection.\nseen_ips = [\"10.0.0.5\", \"10.0.0.5\", \"203.0.113.44\", \"10.0.0.5\"]\nunique = set(seen_ips)\nprint(f\"{len(seen_ips)} sightings -> {len(unique)} unique IPs\")\n\n# Subset test: is every flagged user in the known-user list?\nknown = {\"nadia\", \"rahim\", \"admin\", \"sana\"}\nprint(\"All flagged users known?\", {\"nadia\", \"admin\"} <= known)", output: "Monday users: ['admin', 'nadia', 'rahim']\nBoth days: ['admin', 'rahim']\nNew on Tuesday: ['sana']\nOnly one day: ['nadia', 'sana']\nWas 'guest' seen? False\n4 sightings -> 2 unique IPs\nAll flagged users known? True", lines: ["monday was written with 'nadia' twice and holds her once — uniqueness is enforced by the type, not by your care.", "sorted() wraps every printed set: a set's own order is not a promise, so reports impose one.", "tuesday - monday is the NEW-arrivals question in a single operator — order matters: a - b is not b - a.", "{'nadia', 'admin'} <= known asks the subset question directly — no loop, no counter."] },
