    objectives: ["Use the _name convention to mark internal attributes and control access through methods", "Implement __contains__, __len__, and __repr__ so your objects behave like built-ins", "Use @dataclass for honest data-carrying classes", "Explain that Python encapsulation is convention + design, not locks"],
    relevance: "Well-behaved objects feel native: 'ip' in watchlist, len(watchlist), print(watchlist) — all powered by dunder methods you write. Encapsulation keeps a class's invariants (only valid IPs enter the watchlist) enforceable in one place. Dataclasses remove the boilerplate from the record-shaped classes you will write constantly.",
    concept: [
      "Encapsulation means the class controls the doors: internal attributes are named with a leading underscore (_ips) — a convention meaning 'internal; use the methods' — and the public methods enforce the rules (add() validates the IPv4 shape before storing). Python does not lock attributes with keywords the way some languages do; the underscore plus disciplined callers is the mechanism, and name-mangled __double_underscore exists for the rare hard case.",
      "Dunder methods (double-underscore) are how Python's own syntax reaches your objects: __contains__ powers 'x' in obj, __len__ powers len(obj), __repr__ decides what print shows for debugging, __eq__ decides ==. Implementing them makes a class feel like a built-in type — the reader uses familiar syntax and the class supplies the meaning.",
      "@dataclass is a decorator (next lesson's topic, used here) that writes __init__, __repr__, and __eq__ for a class whose job is to HOLD data: you declare fields with types, and the boilerplate is generated. An Alert becomes three honest lines. Use dataclasses for records, hand-written classes for behaviour-heavy types — the same dict-vs-class judgement, one level up.",
    ],
    diagramTitle: "Making an object feel native",
    diagram: ["_ips                 — internal by convention; the methods are the doors", "add() validates      — invariants enforced in ONE place", "__contains__/__len__ — 'in' and len() work on YOUR object", "@dataclass           — __init__/__repr__/__eq__ written for you"],
    terms: [
      { term: "Encapsulation", def: "Bundling data with controlled access: internals marked _, doors are methods." },
      { term: "Dunder method", def: "A double-underscore method (__len__, __repr__) that hooks Python syntax." },
      { term: "__repr__", def: "The method producing an object's unambiguous string form — what print shows." },
      { term: "Dataclass", def: "A class decorated with @dataclass: field declarations generate init/repr/eq." },
    ],
    code: { lang: "python", title: "oop3.py — complete, runnable (Python 3.10+)", code: "from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Alert:\n    \"\"\"A dataclass writes __init__/__repr__ for you - data, honestly held.\"\"\"\n    user: str\n    count: int\n    source: str\n\na = Alert(\"rahim\", 12, \"203.0.113.44\")\nprint(a)\nprint(\"Alert user:\", a.user)\n\nclass Watchlist:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulation: the set is private; the class controls the doors.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._ips = set()                 # _name = 'internal, use the methods'\n\n    def add(self, ip):\n        parts = ip.split(\".\")\n        if len(parts) != 4:\n            raise ValueError(f\"not an IPv4 shape: {ip}\")\n        self._ips.add(ip)\n\n    def __contains__(self, ip):           # makes 'ip in watchlist' work\n        return ip in self._ips\n\n    def __len__(self):                    # makes len(watchlist) work\n        return len(self._ips)\n\n    def __repr__(self):                   # what print() shows\n        return f\"Watchlist({sorted(self._ips)})\"\n\nw = Watchlist()\nw.add(\"203.0.113.44\")\nw.add(\"10.0.0.5\")\nprint(w)\nprint(\"Watching 10.0.0.5?\", \"10.0.0.5\" in w, \"| size:\", len(w))\ntry:\n    w.add(\"not-an-ip-address\")\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(\"Refused:\", e)", output: "Alert(user='rahim', count=12, source='203.0.113.44')\nAlert user: rahim\nWatchlist(['10.0.0.5', '203.0.113.44'])\nWatching 10.0.0.5? True | size: 2\nRefused: not an IPv4 shape: not-an-ip-address", lines: ["The dataclass printed itself readably with NO __repr__ written — the decorator generated it from the field declarations.", "add() is the only door in: its shape check refused 'not-an-ip-address' — the invariant lives in one method, not in every caller.", "'in' and len() worked on a Watchlist because __contains__ and __len__ defined what they mean for this type.", "print(w) used __repr__: sorted output, unambiguous — the debugging view a developer needs at 2 a.m."] },
    mistakes: ["Treating _private as a security boundary — it is a convention for programmers, not access control against an attacker.", "Writing a full class with hand-rolled __init__/__repr__ for a pure data record — a dataclass is shorter and less error-prone.", "Skipping __repr__ and debugging from '<Watchlist object at 0x...>' printouts."],
    security: "Validated-at-the-door objects keep bad data out of the core: an invalid IP can never sit inside the watchlist, so downstream code trusts the contents. That is input validation expressed as class design — the exceptions lesson, made structural.",
    pc: ["Run oop3.py, then add __iter__ to Watchlist so 'for ip in w' works, and print each entry.", "Convert the Host class from OOP 1 into a @dataclass and note which methods you still had to write yourself."],
    android: ["Android: dataclasses need Python 3.7+; every modern interpreter app qualifies."],
    lab: "Guided lab: in the Playground, write a dataclass Event(user, action, count) and a class Bag with __len__ and __contains__ over a list; demonstrate both.",
    mission: "Mission: name one invariant in a marks-record class (0..100) and the method that should enforce it.",
    practical: "Practical assessment: predict in/len/print results for a dunder-equipped class, and choose dataclass vs hand-written class for two cases.",
    quiz: [
      { q: "'ip' in watchlist works because the class defines:", options: ["__init__", "__contains__ — the dunder behind the 'in' operator", "A method named contains (no underscores)", "It works on every object automatically"], answer: 1, why: "Python syntax hooks are dunder methods; 'in' dispatches to __contains__." },
      { q: "The underscore in self._ips means:", options: ["The attribute is encrypted", "A convention: internal — callers should use the class's methods, not touch it directly", "It is a syntax error to read it", "It is a class attribute"], answer: 1, why: "Encapsulation in Python is convention + design, honestly understood — not a lock." },
      { q: "@dataclass generates:", options: ["A database table", "__init__, __repr__, and __eq__ from declared fields — boilerplate for data classes", "A GUI", "Tests"], answer: 1, why: "Declare the fields; the standard methods are written for you." },
    ],
    summary: "Underscore marks internals, methods enforce the rules, dunders make objects feel native, and dataclasses erase record boilerplate. Next: iterators and generators — lazy data.",
    skillGained: "Encapsulation, dunder methods, and dataclasses.",
