    relevance: "Every log line carries a timestamp; every incident is a timeline. 'How long between the first failure and the lockout?', 'which event came first?', 'when does this certificate expire?' — datetime is how those questions become arithmetic instead of eyeballing. Text sorts wrong across months; datetimes never do.",
    concept: [
      "datetime.strptime(text, fmt) parses text into a datetime using a format string of %-codes: %Y year, %m month, %d day, %H:%M:%S time, %b month name ('Nov'). The format must mirror the source EXACTLY — a syslog line ('Nov 15 09:41:07') and an ISO line ('2026-11-15 09:41:07') need different formats, and guessing wrong raises ValueError (which your exception skills now handle specifically).",
      "Subtracting two datetimes yields a timedelta: (end - start).total_seconds() is the gap as a number — the brute-force window, the session length, the time-to-detect. Adding a timedelta moves a moment: start + timedelta(days=7) is a deadline; comparisons (<, >) order moments directly, which is why parsing BEFORE sorting is the rule: sorted datetimes are chronologically true, sorted timestamp STRINGS are only accidentally true within one format and one month-width.",
      "strftime is strptime's mirror: datetime -> text in the shape your audience needs ('%d %b %Y, %H:%M' for a report, '%A' for the weekday). One professional caution this lesson states plainly: naive datetimes carry no timezone. Logs from two timezones compared naively are a timeline built on sand — record the zone with the timestamp whenever the source provides one (later levels' IR work depends on it).",
    ],
