    code: { lang: "python", title: "datetime_lesson.py — complete, runnable (Python 3.10+)", code: "from datetime import datetime, timedelta\n\n# strptime: text -> datetime, using the format the log actually uses.\nfmt = \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\"\nstart = datetime.strptime(\"2026-11-15 09:41:07\", fmt)\nend = datetime.strptime(\"2026-11-15 09:47:52\", fmt)\nprint(\"Parsed:\", start)\nprint(\"Gap:\", end - start, \"=\", (end - start).total_seconds(), \"seconds\")\n\n# timedelta arithmetic: deadlines, windows, expiries.\nprint(\"7 days later:\", start + timedelta(days=7))\nprint(\"90 minutes before:\", start - timedelta(minutes=90))\n\n# strftime: datetime -> text, in the shape your report needs.\nprint(\"Report shape:\", start.strftime(\"%d %b %Y, %H:%M\"))\nprint(\"Weekday:\", start.strftime(\"%A\"))\n\n# Sorting real events: parse first, then sort the datetimes, not the text.\nevents = [\"2026-11-15 10:02:00\", \"2026-11-15 09:41:07\", \"2026-11-15 09:58:30\"]\ntimes = sorted(datetime.strptime(e, fmt) for e in events)\nprint(\"Earliest:\", times[0], \"| Latest:\", times[-1])", output: "Parsed: 2026-11-15 09:41:07\nGap: 0:06:45 = 405.0 seconds\n7 days later: 2026-11-22 09:41:07\n90 minutes before: 2026-11-15 08:11:07\nReport shape: 15 Nov 2026, 09:41\nWeekday: Sunday\nEarliest: 2026-11-15 09:41:07 | Latest: 2026-11-15 10:02:00", lines: ["The gap prints as 0:06:45 AND as 405.0 seconds — the timedelta for humans, total_seconds() for thresholds and comparisons.", "Adding timedeltas is how deadlines are computed: patch windows, password expiries, certificate renewals.", "strftime('%A') answered 'Sunday' — a fact no string operation on '2026-11-15' could give you.", "The events were sorted as DATETIMES via a generator of strptime calls — parse first, sort second, always."] },
    mistakes: ["Parsing with the wrong format and treating the ValueError as 'bad data' — usually the FORMAT is wrong, not the log.", "Sorting timestamp strings across formats or month boundaries ('Nov' vs 'Dec' as text) and building a false timeline.", "Comparing naive datetimes from sources in different timezones as if they shared one clock."],
    security: "Timelines win investigations, and timelines are datetime arithmetic: attack windows, dwell time, time-to-detect. A timezone-naive comparison can place the response BEFORE the alert — an error that destroys a report's credibility.",
