    code: { lang: "bash", title: "venv_session.sh — the full workflow, on your own PC (Windows notes inline)", code: "# 1. Create the environment (a folder named .venv inside your project)\npython -m venv .venv\n\n# 2. Activate it  (Windows: .venv\\Scripts\\activate)\nsource .venv/bin/activate\n\n# 3. Install a package - into THIS environment only\npip install requests\n\n# 4. See the shelf, then freeze it into the shareable file\npip list\npip freeze > requirements.txt\n\n# 5. Leave the environment when you are done\ndeactivate", output: "(.venv) $ pip list\nPackage    Version\n---------- -------\ncertifi    2026.x\ncharset-normalizer 3.x\nidna       3.x\nrequests   2.x\nurllib3    2.x\n(.venv) $  <- the prompt prefix proves the venv is active", lines: ["Step 1 builds the folder; nothing is installed globally — delete .venv and the project vanishes cleanly.", "The (.venv) prompt prefix is the activation proof: python and pip now resolve INSIDE the project folder.", "requests pulled its own dependencies (urllib3, certifi, idna, charset-normalizer) — freeze records ALL of them, versions included.", "requirements.txt, not the .venv folder, is what you share or commit — the shelf list, not the shelf."] },
    mistakes: ["Installing packages globally 'to keep it simple' and breaking a working tool with the next upgrade.", "Committing or zipping the .venv folder to share a project — share requirements.txt; venvs are machine-local and huge.", "Typing a package name from memory and installing a typosquat — copy names from the project's documentation."],
    security: "A venv is isolation, and pip is a supply chain: every install is code execution by consent. Deliberate names, per-project environments, and reviewed requirements files are the baseline hygiene the cloud and CI/CD lessons will assume.",
