    code: { lang: "python", title: "testing.py — complete, runnable (Python 3.10+)", code: "import unittest\n\ndef classify_port(port):\n    if port in (22, 443, 80):\n        return \"standard\"\n    if port in (21, 23, 3389):\n        return \"risky\"\n    return \"unknown\"\n\n# assert: a one-line check that screams the moment it breaks.\nassert classify_port(22) == \"standard\"\nprint(\"assert passed - the quick check held\")\n\nclass TestClassifyPort(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_standard(self):\n        self.assertEqual(classify_port(443), \"standard\")\n\n    def test_risky(self):\n        self.assertEqual(classify_port(23), \"risky\")\n\n    def test_unknown(self):\n        self.assertEqual(classify_port(9999), \"unknown\")\n\n    def test_zero_is_not_standard(self):\n        self.assertNotEqual(classify_port(0), \"standard\")\n\nsuite = unittest.TestLoader().loadTestsFromTestCase(TestClassifyPort)\nresult = unittest.TextTestRunner(verbosity=2).run(suite)\nprint(\"All tests passed?\", result.wasSuccessful())", output: "test_risky (__main__.TestClassifyPort.test_risky) ... ok\ntest_standard (__main__.TestClassifyPort.test_standard) ... ok\ntest_unknown (__main__.TestClassifyPort.test_unknown) ... ok\ntest_zero_is_not_standard (__main__.TestClassifyPort.test_zero_is_not_standard) ... ok\nRan 4 tests in 0.001s - OK\nassert passed - the quick check held\nAll tests passed? True", lines: ["The assert is the smoke check: one line, run at import, dead the moment the claim breaks.", "Each test method names ONE behaviour — when test_risky fails months from now, its name is the bug report's first sentence.", "Tests run in alphabetical order (risky, standard, unknown, zero) — order is the runner's, and tests must never depend on it.", "wasSuccessful() converts the report into a boolean — the value a CI step or a release checklist acts on."] },
    mistakes: ["Using assert to validate USER input — python -O strips asserts; hostile input meets no check at all.", "Writing one giant test method for ten claims — the first failure hides the other nine verdicts.", "Testing that a function 'runs' instead of testing what it RETURNS for the edge cases (0, empty, the risky one)."],
    security: "Parsers and validators are security boundaries; their edge cases are the attack surface. A test suite that pins the boundary cases (empty, oversized, the known-bad value) is a control — it makes silent weakening of a check visible the day it happens.",
    pc: ["Run testing.py, then deliberately break classify_port (make 23 'standard') and watch exactly which tests fail and what they print.", "Write tests for read_port from the exceptions lesson: '443' accepts, 'ssh' raises InvalidPortError (assertRaises), '70000' raises."],
    android: ["Android: unittest runs in phone interpreters; the suite above is small enough to practise there."],
    lab: "Guided lab: in the Playground, write a TestCase for a function is_risky(port) with tests for 23 (True), 443 (False), and 21 (True) — run the suite programmatically as in the lesson.",
    mission: "Mission: name the three claims you would test FIRST for a password-strength function, and why those three.",
