    code: { lang: "python", title: "requests_lesson.py — complete, runnable on your PC (Python 3.10+, needs requests)", code: "import json\nimport threading\nfrom http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, HTTPServer\n\nimport requests\n\n# A tiny JSON server of your own (in a thread) - the target is localhost.\nclass Handler(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n    def do_GET(self):\n        body = json.dumps({\"service\": \"lab-app\", \"version\": 1}).encode()\n        self.send_response(200)\n        self.send_header(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\")\n        self.send_header(\"X-Lab\", \"training\")\n        self.end_headers()\n        self.wfile.write(body)\n\n    def log_message(self, *args):\n        pass                                     # keep the output clean\n\nserver = HTTPServer((\"127.0.0.1\", 0), Handler)\nthreading.Thread(target=server.serve_forever, daemon=True).start()\nurl = f\"http://127.0.0.1:{server.server_address[1]}/status\"\n\nwith requests.Session() as session:              # a session reuses connections\n    r = session.get(url, timeout=5)              # timeout: always, no exceptions\n    print(\"Status:\", r.status_code)\n    print(\"JSON as Python:\", r.json())\n    print(\"X-Lab header:\", r.headers.get(\"X-Lab\"))\n    print(\"Missing header:\", r.headers.get(\"Strict-Transport-Security\", \"MISSING\"))\n    r.raise_for_status()                         # raises for 4xx/5xx - none here\nprint(\"Session closed cleanly; server was your own, on 127.0.0.1\")\nserver.shutdown()", output: "Status: 200\nJSON as Python: {'service': 'lab-app', 'version': 1}\nX-Lab header: training\nMissing header: MISSING\nSession closed cleanly; server was your own, on 127.0.0.1", lines: ["The server is the lesson's own, on an OS-picked port — the requests code is unchanged from what you would run against your training app.", "r.json() returned a DICT: the body became data at the border, ready for the nested-navigation skills from the JSON lesson.", ".get('Strict-Transport-Security', 'MISSING') is the header-audit idiom: absence is a finding with a default, not a crash.", "raise_for_status() sat after the prints as a gate: a 500 here would have raised HTTPError instead of letting an error page pose as data."] },
    mistakes: ["Treating r.text (an error page's HTML) as API data when the status was 500 — check status/raise first, parse second.", "One requests.get per call in a polling loop — no session, no reuse, needless load on your own service.", "Hard-coding an Authorization header for a real service into source code — tokens come from the environment (pathlib/os lesson), never from the file."],
    security: "Header and status analysis is a junior analyst's first deliverable, and its professional shape is this lesson: own targets, timeouts, status discipline, headers checked by name, bodies handled as data. The same code against someone else's service without authorisation is not analysis — Level 0's rule is part of the tool.",
    pc: ["Run requests_lesson.py on your PC (inside the venv with requests installed), then add a /missing path request in a try/except and print the HTTPError for the 404.", "Point the session at python -m http.server on localhost and audit which of the four Level 5 headers it sends."],
    android: ["Android: Termux can run Python + requests on the same network as your lab — same code, same authorisation rule."],
    lab: "Guided lab: on your PC, extend the lesson's server with a /headers endpoint that echoes request headers as JSON, then print from the client which User-Agent requests sends by default.",
